    "case_id": "507",
    "case_summary": "An 83-year-old man presented with months of fatigue and a 20-pound unintentional weight loss. Medical history includes: two instances of colon cancer treated with surgery and 5-fluorouracil in the 1970s, squamous cell carcinoma of the right palatine tonsil treated with radiation, squamous cell carcinoma and basal cell carcinoma of the skin. Family history notable for various cancers without clear genetic cause. Initial laboratory evaluation revealed new pancytopenia, which later showed recovery of all cell lines.\n\nNo specific physical examination findings were detailed in the case report.\n\n- Laboratory Tests:    * Initial bone marrow biopsy: No abnormal clonal populations    * Complete blood count: Initially showed pancytopenia with subsequent recovery    * Peripheral blood smear: No peripheral blasts or abnormal B or T cells    * Flow cytometry of tissue blasts: Positive for CD13, CD34, CD38, and CD117    * Repeat bone marrow biopsy: Negative for proliferative disorders- Imaging Studies:    * CT scan of neck, chest, abdomen, and pelvis: Showed only patchy enhancement of right palatine tonsil    * PET-CT: Revealed hypermetabolic cervical lymph nodes and an ill-defined focus with intense hypermetabolism (SUV 11.8) near the inferior right atrioventricular groove    * Gated CT angiography: Mass encasing the mid-right coronary artery, measuring 3.0 cm x 2.4 cm x 1.8 cm    * Cardiac MRI: Mass measuring 9.6 cm AP x 6.9 cm TV x 3.8 cm CC with heterogeneous signal intensity on T1- and T2-weighted images and heterogeneous postcontrast enhancement    * Transthoracic echocardiogram: Mass within the right atrium, causing restricted anterior tricuspid leaflet motion and mild tricuspid stenosis    * Selective coronary angiography: Excluded compression of the right coronary artery and significant angiographic disease    - Pathology:    * Fine needle aspiration of cervical lymph node: Normal reactive tissue    * Endomyocardial biopsy: 80% tissue blast cells",
